From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Foliage takes light without casting a quad

Patch 0012 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The ladder in patch 0011 did its job. The user, 2026-09-30: "solid if reads depth is what makes
navis light appear properly". So this game's foliage READS depth and never writes it, which is
also why the rule that tested for a depth WRITE did nothing at all, and why patch 0010's coverage
and alpha compare test never matched either.

Reading depth is too wide a rule on its own, and his next sentence says exactly how: "it has
harsh traiangular shadows and also has a weird mostly transparent triangular outline that its
likely what is casting the shadow". Every glow, spark and shaft in the game is drawn in that same
render mode, so all of them became solid too, and a transparent quad traced as solid casts the
shadow of a quad. The outline and the shadow are the same two triangles seen twice.

Two ways to narrow it, both on the Experiment row so the picture decides rather than another
reading of the code. Exclude the sprites by triangle count, since a billboard is two triangles
and a tree is many. And let a reclassified draw take light WITHOUT casting it: a new instance
mask that every gathering ray traces and the shadow ray does not. Only the draws a rule
reclassified lose their shadow, so geometry that was always opaque is untouched and nothing the
game already shadowed stops shadowing.

No shadow at all is not the end state. A leaf should cast a leaf, which means the alpha test at
the hit on the shadow ray, and that is a later change worth making once the discriminator is
settled. Until then a missing shadow is better than a wrong one.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/common/rt64_common.h b/src/common/rt64_common.h
--- a/src/common/rt64_common.h
+++ b/src/common/rt64_common.h
@@ -33,6 +33,15 @@ namespace RT64 {
     // A translucent draw (the microcode's XLU z mode), which no ray traces (patch 0006): a
     // flame's billboard would otherwise stand between every surface and its torch.
     static const unsigned int TranslucentRayQueryMask = 0x8;
+    // FOLIAGE THE RAYS LIGHT BUT NO SHADOW RAY SEES (patch 0012). A draw reclassified as
+    // solid by one of the cut-out rules can be given this instead of the depth mask: every
+    // gathering ray traces it, so the surface takes light and occludes like geometry, and the
+    // shadow ray does not, so it casts nothing. The reason is that this game draws its
+    // foliage as quads: traced as a shadow caster, a two triangle quad casts a two triangle
+    // shadow, which is the hard triangular darkening the user reported on 2026-09-30. A leaf
+    // shaped shadow needs the alpha test on the shadow ray, which is a later change; until
+    // then no shadow is better than a wrong one.
+    static const unsigned int FoliageRayQueryMask = 0x10;
 
     // Error string for last error or exception that was caught.
     extern std::string GlobalLastError;
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -33,6 +33,10 @@ static const uint DepthRayQueryMask = 0x1;
 static const uint NoDepthRayQueryMask = 0x2;
 static const uint ShadowCatcherRayQueryMask = 0x4;
 static const uint TranslucentRayQueryMask = 0x8;   // patch 0006: the XLU z mode, which only the water probe traces
+static const uint FoliageRayQueryMask = 0x10;      // patch 0012: lit and occluding, but no shadow ray sees it
+// What every gathering ray traces: the depth masked geometry and the foliage. The SHADOW ray
+// deliberately leaves the foliage out, which is the whole point of the separate bit.
+static const uint GatherRayQueryMask = DepthRayQueryMask | FoliageRayQueryMask;
 
 // How much nearer than the rasterized depth a primary hit may be before it counts as a cut-out
 // the raster did not draw (patch 0008), in the depth buffer's own units.
@@ -157,7 +161,7 @@ void PrimaryRayGen() {
     // call with depth test and write both off is the game painting a backdrop, and this game
     // draws its sky as a box around the eye that way, which would otherwise stand in front of
     // every ray. The no-depth instances are the background passes' concern.
-    TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, DepthRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
+    TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, GatherRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
 
     gViewDirection[launchIndex] = float4(direction, 0.0f);
 
@@ -214,7 +218,7 @@ void PrimaryRayGen() {
             payload.shadedColor = 0.0f;
             payload.hitVelocity = 0.0f;
             payload.coverage = 1.0f;
-            TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, DepthRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
+            TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, GatherRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
             // Nothing further along the ray at all: the picture shows what the rays cannot see.
             depthDropped = (payload.instanceId < 0);
         }
@@ -1014,7 +1018,7 @@ void IndirectRayGen() {
     payload.shadedColor = 0.0f;
     payload.hitVelocity = 0.0f;
     payload.coverage = 1.0f;
-    TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, DepthRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
+    TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, GatherRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
 
     // A ray that leaves the scene brings nothing: the sky's light is what the game's ambient
     // already is, and adding the horizon's color again washed the open field out.
@@ -1065,7 +1069,7 @@ void ReflectionRayGen() {
     payload.shadedColor = 0.0f;
     payload.hitVelocity = 0.0f;
     payload.coverage = 1.0f;
-    TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, DepthRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
+    TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, GatherRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, ray, payload);
 
     float3 radiance;
     if (payload.instanceId >= 0) {
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1836,9 +1836,29 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         //            not the classification at all and the search moves to the
                         //            raster order and the depth mask. It will be wrong for water
                         //            and for every glow, which is the point: it is a probe.
+                        // THE SECOND RUNG (patch 0012). "Solid if reads depth" is the rule that
+                        // works: the user, 2026-09-30, "solid if reads depth is what makes navis
+                        // light appear properly". That tells us this game's foliage READS depth
+                        // and never writes it, which is why "writes depth" did nothing at all.
+                        //
+                        // But it is too wide, and his next sentence says how: "it has harsh
+                        // traiangular shadows and also has a weird mostly transparent triangular
+                        // outline that its likely what is casting the shadow". Every glow, spark
+                        // and shaft in the game is drawn in that same render mode, so they all
+                        // became solid too, and a transparent QUAD traced as solid casts the
+                        // shadow of a quad. He is right that the outline is what casts it: the
+                        // outline and the shadow are the same two triangles seen twice.
+                        //
+                        // Two ways to narrow it, both on the row so the picture decides again:
+                        // exclude the sprites by their triangle count (a billboard is two
+                        // triangles, a tree is many), and let these draws take light without
+                        // casting it (FoliageRayQueryMask, which every gathering ray traces and
+                        // the shadow ray does not).
                         const uint32_t acMode = otherMode.alphaCompare();
                         const int cutoutRule = int(rtResources->rtParams.tuneExperiment.x);
+                        const bool manyTriangles = (call.callDesc.triangleCount > 2);
                         bool solidDraw;
+                        bool castsNothing = false;
                         switch (cutoutRule) {
                             case 8:  solidDraw = false; break;
                             case 9:  solidDraw = otherMode.zUpd(); break;
@@ -1846,12 +1866,22 @@ namespace RT64 {
                             case 11: solidDraw = otherMode.alphaCvgSel(); break;
                             case 12: solidDraw = !otherMode.forceBlend(); break;
                             case 13: solidDraw = true; break;
+                            case 14: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp(); castsNothing = true; break;
+                            case 15: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
+                            case 16: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; castsNothing = true; break;
                             default: solidDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER); break;
                         }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
-                        if ((otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !solidDraw) {
+                        const bool wasTranslucent = (otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU);
+                        if (wasTranslucent && !solidDraw) {
                             raytracing.queryMask = TranslucentRayQueryMask;
                         }
+                        else if (wasTranslucent && castsNothing) {
+                            // Only the draws this rule RECLASSIFIED lose their shadow. Geometry
+                            // that was always opaque is untouched, so nothing the game already
+                            // shadowed stops shadowing.
+                            raytracing.queryMask = FoliageRayQueryMask;
+                        }
                         else {
                             raytracing.queryMask = (otherMode.zCmp() || otherMode.zUpd()) ? DepthRayQueryMask : NoDepthRayQueryMask;
                         }
